W6.note:
The K1 statement is qualified accurately:
- the entries are declared data;
- LoaderEntrySelected is a volatile, runtime-writable variable (systemd v256 save_selected_entry);
- the source is the /proc/cmdline text, which is saved_command_line, including bootconfig keys and init arguments;
- nothing is measured.
The maintenance token is pinned and the lockdown test stops at "--" (V47-V49). But the token rule does not model how systemd reads the command line: it misses tokens after "--", ignores quoting and ignores last-wins (R2-1). The positives remain only partly loader-consistent (R2-2).

questionAnswers.Q2:
Every v2 rule still holds:
- All 131 v2 negatives replay.
- Exactly R70, R71, L13 and Q07 change for a reason. R01's change is mechanical and is now noted.
- C14-C18 and C20 only add threadCount 1 and an unenrolled image. Q03 uses a v3 capture.
- The four v2 accepted variants replay. A02 now also sets sliceDescendants to 6.
The v1 migration guarantees of v2 are restored.
The identity-disjointness rule is still subsumed: no enum name is shared, the KUBELET pattern matches no other name, and the new node-count rule does not change that.
Legitimate hosts accepted:
- A05, A06 and A07;
- the gate on another device with the same inode number;
- libpython shared by SERVER and WORKER under the tree;
- the kubelet with one node name plus system:nodes;
- the systemd-boot initrd-first order.
The schema's nlink const 1 makes the inode rule consistent with real hosts.
Legitimate configurations refused:
- a MAINTENANCE entry whose systemd.unit= comes after "--" (bootconfig init.*);
- a quoted lockdown parameter (both R2-1);
- a role cgroup read with an unreaped task, which inspector obligation 3 is meant to exclude.

questionAnswers.Q4:
Yes, as a loader-reported, unmeasured discriminator. The entry IDs and command lines must differ, and every role and lifecycle capture must report the enrolled entry ID and the SHA-256 of its /proc/cmdline text. A boot through the MAINTENANCE entry therefore cannot qualify (V39, V40).
The README statement is accurate:
- the entries are declared;
- LoaderEntrySelected is exported volatile and is runtime-writable;
- /proc/cmdline is fixed for the boot and includes bootconfig keys and init arguments;
- nothing is measured, and measurement is a T04 obligation;
- the maintenance domain is a trusted installer.
The statement is sufficient for K1's 'explicit statement' alternative. It is not complete on two points, neither of which affects telling an honest maintenance boot apart:
- the new maintenance-token rule does not model how systemd reads the command line, so an ENROLLED line can select the maintenance target and still pass (R2-1);
- without Secure Boot, systemd's default target can also come from the SystemdOptions EFI variable, unless the command line names its own unit.

questionAnswers.Q5:
Refused as intended:
- the gate at the broker's inode;
- the server script at the python inode;
- python at two inodes across captures;
- the worker script at the broker's inode;
- a worker co-member at another python inode, or running the gate image;
- a server member whose image differs from the process image;
- python3.11 under the tree in the gate;
- a content copy of the enrolled python3.12 in the observer;
- the kubelet with two node names;
- an extra census entry without a matching nr_descendants;
- every v1/v2 boot or nonce reuse.
Accepted:
- a content copy of the tree's libpython outside the tree in the gate (R2-3);
- python3.11 outside the tree (stated residual);
- interpreted roles running any script (stated: declared, not observed);
- a fabricated unpopulated census entry with a matching nr_descendants (inspector honesty);
- ENROLLED lines carrying the maintenance token after "--" or after a quoted " -- " (R2-1).
Beyond inspector-honesty limits, I found no way for a bundle to carry its own observation of a foreign cgroup, process or program and still qualify. Cgroup paths and inodes, (pid, start ticks) and program IDs stay bound and unique.

questionAnswers.Q6:
Each case produces exactly its pinned result:
- the 4 positives are accepted with test_fixture=True, including with full, v1-only, v2-only or attested-empty histories, and are refused 'test-only implementation profile' without it;
- 182/182 negatives, of which 49 are schema-level refusals, each a single error;
- 7/7 accepted variants;
- 14/14 cross-version cases;
- 13/13 migration cases.
The intents of V43-V51 match the rules that fire. My harness rebuilds every case from fresh JSON copies, so the withdrawn shared-object problem cannot hide a result.
The positives are kernel-plausible in the new parts:
- the gate has its own inode, and every inode names one path, with nlink 1;
- each member's image equals the inspected process's image;
- the worker's pids.current equals its members' threads;
- sliceDescendants 7 equals the 7 listed descendants.
Remaining implausibilities:
- initrd= is placed after the options (R2-2);
- python3.12's sha256 string equals the proxy-serve script's verity string. This is inherited from v2 and harmless, because the model keys sha256 and verity separately.
Vector weaknesses are in R2-4.

questionAnswers.Q7:
Nothing claims a native observation, a measured boot, a selected distribution, seccomp filters or an E01-E12 proof. The 'Not claimed' section is intact.
Wording that claims more than the bytes support:
- the model comment 'the enrolled entry never does', and the K1 row's implied property (R2-1);
- 'its options line plus the initrd= it adds', next to positives that use the wrong order (R2-2);
- 'not recognised by the data', which does not fit content copies (R2-3);
- 'each pinned to its exact refused rule' (R2-4);
- 'is carried ... in this contract's status record' (R2-6).

